the u.s has long accused china of cyber  spying but it's never made those  accusations official in a court of law  until now attorney general eric holder's  message enough is enough this  administration will not tolerate actions  by any nation that seeks to illegally  sabotage american companies and  undermine the integrity of fair  competition in the operation of the free  market  this case should serve as a wake-up call  to the seriousness of the ongoing cyber  threat  the indictments are against five members  of the chinese military they allegedly  spied on six u.s companies including  nuclear power plant builder westinghouse  and alcoa the largest american aluminum  company  the u.s says the alleged spying gave the  chinese companies unfair advantages in  the market  the chinese foreign ministry quickly  rejected the charges and said the case  would harm u.s chinese relations  china is a staunch defender of network  security it said in a statement and the  chinese government military and  associated personnel have never engaged  in online theft of trade secrets  the charges against the six defendants  could carry decades in prison  but they're all at large in china and  the u.s acknowledges there's virtually  no chance of them being extradited
 

